; Shoals over every shelf and every stretch of open water on the planet, not one
; bay's worth: the schools are sited on the sea floor around wherever the viewer
; is and re-sited as they travel, so the ocean is populated wherever you swim out
; into it. Each is its own MultiMesh, so the ones you are not near cost nothing:
; the bodies are read entirely from the VAT shader, and the script only re-poses
; the shoals close enough to be watched.
;
; Sixty-four schools over a six-hundred-metre ring is about the density the bay
; used to hold on its own. What it costs to draw is set by draw_within and not by
; the ring, so widening the ring is only worth doing alongside more schools.
[node name="FishSchools" type="Node3D" parent="Planet"]
script = ExtResource("16_school")
model = ExtResource("17_fish_model")
material = ExtResource("18_fish_material")
vat = ExtResource("19_fish_vat")
instance_count = 3200
cluster_count = 64
scatter_radius = 620.0
cluster_radius = 2.6
cruise_speed = 3.6
school_orbit = 15.0
simulate_within = 220.0
draw_within = 340.0
pose_near_within = 65.0
pose_rate_near = 45.0
pose_rate_far = 15.0
max_pose_updates_per_frame = 4
avoid_reach = 4.5
avoid_push = 3.4

; The reef swarms: sixty-four bait balls of seven hundred and fifty, each one its
; own MultiMesh and its own draw call, holding station two and a half metres
; over the same sand the coral is rooted in. Neither node knows about the other
; — they meet because a school told to hug a bed between eight and thirty
; metres down is over the band the corals grow in, and both read that band off
; the same height field.
;
; Which is now true of every reef on the planet rather than the one off the
; beach. The coral is global cover and always was; these swarms used to be a fan
; pinned to Vacationer's Landing, so the two agreed only where they happened to
; overlap. Siting the swarms around the viewer instead makes the depth band they
; share do the work everywhere: swim out over any sand eight to thirty metres
; down and there is coral, and there are fish over it.
;
; Five hundred fish in a ball is affordable for the reason the whole VAT pipe
; exists: the body is a texture read in the vertex shader, so a fish costs a
; hundred and fifty triangles and sixteen floats in a buffer, and the swarm
; costs one call. Schools remain visible farther out because their VAT keeps
; swimming with no CPU work; only the nearby ones are re-posed around their
; orbit, which is what simulate_within is for.
[node name="ReefSwarms" type="Node3D" parent="Planet"]
